📋 Overview
This repository/log documents a progressive Nmap reconnaissance workflow performed against a target Linux host (10.113.155.142). The exercise demonstrates how to systematically escalate scan depth, extract service versions, handle verbosity, apply timing templates, run the Nmap Scripting Engine (NSE), and capture output formats for reporting and analysis.

🛠️ Step-by-Step Command Execution & Methodology
1. Initial Service Version Detection (-sV)
To identify the versions of services listening on open ports without running a full noisy scan, service version detection (-sV) was executed.

Bash
nmap -sV 10.113.155.142
2. Increasing Verbosity (-v / -vv)
To troubleshoot scan stalls and capture real-time state changes and reason codes (e.g., syn-ack ttl 64), verbosity was ramped up to level two (-vv):

Bash
nmap -sV -vv 10.113.155.142
3. Saving Output Formats (-oA / -oN / -oG)
To persist the findings for documentation and automated parsing, results were saved using multiple output flags:

All Formats (-oA):

Bash
nmap -sV -vv -oA target_scan 10.113.155.142
Normal Format (-oN):

Bash
nmap -sV -vv -oN scan_results.txt 10.113.155.142
Greppable Format (-oG):

Bash
nmap -sV -vv -oG scan_results.gnmap 10.113.155.142
4. Targeting Specific Ports (-p)
To verify specific ports or restrict scope (such as auditing specific services or checking web availability like port 80):

Specific Port Lists:

Bash
nmap -p 22,53,80,8443 10.113.155.142
Single Port Check (Port 80):

Bash
nmap -p 80 10.113.155.142
5. Aggressive Scan & Scripting Engine (NSE) (-A, -T5, --script=vuln)
To execute a comprehensive audit covering OS fingerprinting, default scripts, vulnerability checks, and accelerated timing templates:

Bash
sudo nmap -T5 -sV -vv -A --script=vuln 10.113.155.142
